update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('d09bbec0-51eb-49df-89af-535df224ddeb'::uuid,'202bca7fd1ee26a02c56a48c4228f2ad','This treats the ancestral species as a lemur. The passage says higher primates are thought to have evolved from one diurnal lower-primate species, and that certain lemurs are the only living diurnal lower primates. The ancestor could be an extinct species that was never a lemur. And "thought to have evolved" is a belief, not an established fact, so no claim about chimpanzee ancestry has to be true.'),
('d64c48e3-e2d5-4ae0-991c-bb04e630db40'::uuid,'f938d591ee4ed832f38d825bd99cb4d2','This is the chain from the Goal. Lemurs are the only primates indigenous to Madagascar, and lemurs are lower primates, so every primate native to Madagascar is a lower primate and none is a higher primate. B narrows that to "diurnal higher primates," which changes nothing. If no higher primate is native to Madagascar, no diurnal higher primate is either.

The move to carry forward: when an answer adds a qualifier to a "none" statement you''ve proven, check whether the qualifier only shrinks the group. A smaller slice of an excluded group is still excluded.'),
('e667bfa1-7239-4f50-87cf-0a48170a4d22'::uuid,'199d8ea367675614c6dfc7fa8433458c','The passage describes the ancestor, not the descendants. Even if every higher primate came from a diurnal species, nothing stops a descendant species from being nocturnal today. The passage never says when higher primates are active now, so "no higher primate is nocturnal" doesn''t have to be true.'),
('ea62f47c-ca93-4465-a834-b51fde78e899'::uuid,'a74d53ada6c69dfc6007263adcbc5cba','This is the belief the objection runs on. The sociologists reject the theory because a complex phenomenon can''t be caused by a simple one. That only counts against rational-choice theory if the cause the theory names, voters'' economically motivated decisions, is simple. So the sociologists must think those decisions are not necessarily complex. "Need not constitute a complex phenomenon" says exactly that, and says it modestly.

The move: when a group rejects a theory by citing a general rule, ask which category the rule must put the theory''s key idea in. That classification is something the group has to believe.'),
('91303727-b979-40ca-97e8-03e3c45302de'::uuid,'75dd551d62e04ed1f735e69a88af93ba','The sociologists'' rule only rules one thing out: a simple cause can''t produce a complex result. It says nothing about how many causes a complex phenomenon has, or that each of them must be complex. A single complex cause would fit their rule just as well. "Many complex causes" adds a claim about number that we can''t trace to anything the sociologists said.'),
('d0fbbc49-2a5e-41dc-b342-a1693f9f0d03'::uuid,'17262829941900edf0f13b28582e746a','The sociologists never say what does cause popular support for parties. They only say the theory''s cause is too simple. Religion and culture never appear in the passage, so we have no basis for crediting the sociologists with this view.'),
('d598225c-6950-457b-9118-8100a31a6498'::uuid,'cd809889ee9c0da2c82b1dd337fae737','This is the reverse of what they believe. Their rule is about complex phenomena, so the objection only applies if popular support for parties is complex. If they thought it was never complex, their own rule wouldn''t touch the theory and the objection would collapse.'),
('96c1bf06-f611-4656-8b35-6f3266092de7'::uuid,'26d95a27dd8c2122b490206b8c826812','The sociologists don''t deny that voters care about economic advantage. They deny that those decisions are enough, by themselves, to explain something as complex as the rise of a political organization. Someone can agree that voters chase economic advantage and still think it''s too simple a cause.'),
('8087806a-c139-4f86-9acd-24154744ec16'::uuid,'3f03e59b3b68ca9507116c34dcc9726d','The citizen says unemployment is the highest ever, not that the recession is the worst ever. Negate A: this recession is not the worst in the country''s history. The tax-cut plan still works exactly as described, and record unemployment is still a reason to act quickly.'),
('8df43ddc-caab-4df4-94c0-f741a2a344e0'::uuid,'0d6ec3a7aa0572a73b059d388036d123','B is a sliding scale: the bigger the cut, the more likely people invest. The citizen needs only that this particular cut leads to investment. Negate B: a bigger cut does not make investing more likely. This major cut could still produce a large increase in investment, so the argument survives.'),
('0b839eed-266d-4c14-b984-dff421bb2429'::uuid,'6b5df403e978be9eb8637e1be697b7e9','Negate it: upper-income citizens could not use the tax-cut money in any way that increases investment. Then the "correspondingly large increase in investment" can''t happen, the new jobs never come, and the case for the tax cut collapses.

Notice how little D claims. It doesn''t say they will invest, only that they could. That''s typical of a correct Necessary Assumption answer: the argument doesn''t need a guarantee, but it can''t survive if the outcome it predicts is impossible.'),
('bbc83bd7-a43f-448d-8303-87715ee990a2'::uuid,'21ae306bfc3504d47bb1897d69841bbf','"Never" is far more than the argument needs. The scientist''s evidence is about one kind of manipulation, selective breeding. Negate A: some manipulation of genetic codes is unethical, say, manipulation done to cause harm. The scientist can accept that and still argue that genetic engineering, like selective breeding, is not unethical.'),
('24dabea0-eb2c-4347-b6c6-43e836b49b17'::uuid,'c1c716604c8c7c5d5fb37b1f93cea360','B is tempting because it sounds like a defense of genetic engineering. But the scientist''s evidence is selective breeding, something people did, not something nature accomplished. Negate B: some things nature accomplishes would be unethical to accomplish with science. The comparison between breeding and genetic engineering is untouched.'),
('9d05888d-1674-455d-9d01-37cd5e465428'::uuid,'3b256c75c1027e5f163d5dd03c3a7513','Negate it: "selective breeding for desired traits is unethical." Then the analogy turns against the scientist. Showing that genetic engineering is like selective breeding only puts it next to something unethical, and the millennia of practice just mean we''ve been doing something wrong for a long time. The conclusion falls.

The move: in an argument by analogy, the conclusion borrows a property from the comparison case. The argument has to assume the comparison case actually has that property.'),
('710820c3-cac8-4ec5-95cc-9d798666d4e0'::uuid,'d0adadacb4b64104fff6ece2db79dd3f','The argument is about ethics, not usefulness. Negate D: selective breeding is not important for human survival. Breeding could still be ethical, and the analogy still works. Something can be unimportant and perfectly acceptable.'),
('7ca72712-983a-4b7c-9943-b462ce4f7f8c'::uuid,'2c07322d7a891ac13efe90b80109a01f','E makes two sweeping claims: science can do only what is natural, and nothing natural is unethical. The scientist relies on neither. Negate E, say, science can do some things that aren''t natural, and the comparison between selective breeding and genetic engineering still stands.'),
('ab7be3d8-c1dd-47b3-b2ff-b9d7d4a150fc'::uuid,'a5dbbceecf1ea96b8f3e2319a1f2362c','This is the link from the Evaluate step. Negate it: plumper beans do not enhance the quality of a dish. Then skipping the soak buys nothing on the quality side, and the conclusion has no support.

The pattern to repeat: find the idea that appears only in the conclusion (quality), find the idea it replaced in the evidence (plumper beans), and look for the answer that connects them.'),
('0c6d5556-a811-4bc3-baf7-ce13b161f6d5'::uuid,'d60c4389778910fc971ba3ad8597493a','A reverses the relationship the scientist needs. The argument needs ample food and little exercise to be capable of making animals unhealthy. A instead says a restricted diet and plenty of exercise guarantee health. Negate A: animals on that regimen could still be unhealthy for some other reason. The lab conditions could still harm them and skew results, so the argument is untouched.'),
('9afe9cb1-0652-4a44-948f-90b0bac54314'::uuid,'4492bc470b55a0b27eb55bb449170a35','Negate it: "these conditions *cannot* be unhealthy for the animals." Then the animals are healthy, the studies'' assumption holds, and there is nothing to skew the results.

The move: when the evidence describes a condition and the conclusion says it skews results, the argument has to assume the condition actually affects what the studies take for granted. Here, that''s the animals'' health.'),
('9659c2e3-53c6-426d-b6e9-c52b170844b2'::uuid,'11af3bca50681334df5b466b208f1c50','The scientist''s claim is about lab animals and the studies that use them. Negate C: it is unusual for animals outside labs to have ample food and little exercise. That doesn''t change whether lab conditions can make lab animals unhealthy, so the argument stands.'),
('141cd8cd-9fab-4f13-bc3a-c50ea68c1e6d'::uuid,'7e89bd89045844e58438d9be3d6be2d8','D says some studies already account for differences in living conditions. The scientist only claims these factors can skew results. Negate D: no animal studies take those differences into account. The argument still works, so it doesn''t depend on D.'),
('4cfc1100-804b-40eb-a325-b3203c009063'::uuid,'2c87ca9e85c3fc4746976babe8aa6420','E is about how consistent the animals'' eating is from day to day. The argument turns on whether ample food and little exercise can harm their health. Negate E: animals with unlimited food vary a lot from day to day. That tells us nothing about whether they''re healthy, so the argument is unaffected.'),
('250d31d6-6ab0-47cb-b1c0-b63dde4f4320'::uuid,'f3730786483c118843c8a0836f9a969a','The argument is about what highly profitable corporations can do, not about how many corporations do it now. Negate A: many corporations give bonuses strong enough to keep employees working hard without monitoring. That doesn''t hurt the argument at all, so the argument doesn''t depend on A.'),
('c171f899-2ed0-4c1e-b522-b4c733420709'::uuid,'e1aa9ae1fb09d7f61b98b8b0ad54a306','B explains why savings would happen: if bonuses save money, it''s because they cut monitoring time. The argument only needs bonuses to be able to save money, not a claim about the reason behind every case of savings. Negate B: a corporation could save money with bonuses for some other reason. The conclusion still holds, since it only says the corporations can save money.'),
('d59a0789-6a22-45b1-aff3-e95dbc75ce67'::uuid,'d736be7fef8e830db03501d2ca7bf74e','C is about whether these corporations actually have managers monitor much. The argument is about what they could save by reducing monitoring. Negate C: more valuable manager time doesn''t make monitoring less likely. That fits the argument fine. Monitoring is still costly, and cutting it while keeping employees motivated still saves money.'),
('e18b806d-1d05-4ac2-9543-4cccf8e88403'::uuid,'01a77ae1a7d03ba65e28c7e218a5edba','This connects the two terms the argument swaps. The evidence says these corporations can save money by reducing monitoring, as long as employees get strong incentives. The conclusion says they can save money by giving expensive bonuses. D says expensive bonuses are strong incentives for exactly these employees. Negate it: the bonuses aren''t strong incentives. Then the evidence''s condition isn''t met, and nothing in the argument shows the bonuses save any money.

The move: when the conclusion names a specific tool (expensive bonuses) and the evidence names a general requirement (strong incentives), the argument assumes the tool meets the requirement.'),
('831eb0ef-227b-4cbb-9206-4d01df108da7'::uuid,'de018f71bbad7c1780f590bde8bd8d93','E is too strong. "Only if" makes expensive bonuses a requirement for any savings from reduced monitoring. The argument needs bonuses to be one way to supply strong incentives, not the only way. Negate E: a corporation could cut monitoring and save money using other incentives, like promotions. Bonuses could still work too, so the argument stands.'),
('98fb449c-dbe4-4f1b-8241-fcb4e09160e5'::uuid,'53052901acd8da4763ae35330bca20e4','The argument doesn''t care how people come to take a reasoned approach. Its conclusion only says what should follow once most of them do. Negate A: governments alone can convince people to be reasonable. The conditional conclusion is untouched, so the argument doesn''t need A.'),
('cab48aae-47a5-4fa6-a1d7-78a31f45233d'::uuid,'dad8e84af35036d648f0687bbb542f73','B is about a cost of government doing its job: does it get in the way of people pursuing their own welfare? The argument never weighs that cost. Negate B: government can''t serve its purpose without diminishing people''s ability to promote their welfare. The link between reasoned people and less need for government is unaffected.'),
('873db1a2-d32b-47b8-aa8b-77575bbd7d64'::uuid,'e4cd7c8fe8931e47025b1122cd16d39c','This is the link the Goal predicted. Government exists to prevent people from injuring others. The conclusion says government should have less power when most people take a reasoned approach. That only follows if reasoned people injure others less. Negate C: people who take a reasoned approach are just as likely to injure others as anyone else. Then a reasoned population needs government''s protection just as much, and the case for less power falls apart.

The move: find the idea that appears only in the conclusion (a reasoned approach) and the idea it needs to connect to in the evidence (injuring others). The answer that ties them together is usually the required assumption.'),
('a0f691c3-1168-408d-b794-e568158be582'::uuid,'17ee827111702354df4ee794c511c91e','D is about what happens to people who injure others afterward: do they get the same treatment back? The argument is about whether reasoned people need government to stop them from injuring others in the first place. Negate D: people who injure others are not eventually treated the same way. Nothing in the argument changes.'),
('125cc63e-93fe-4f81-81ab-cbb50d35287a'::uuid,'cda480f947fce01b3e4625218815def8','E is about the risk of being injured, not of injuring others. It also ties that risk to how much interest people have in their own welfare, not to whether they take a reasoned approach. Negate E and the argument is untouched: the theorist''s case still turns on reasoned people injuring others less.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;